# Committed template for dashboard-server's media storage credentials.
#
#   cp dashboard-server/.env-example dashboard-server/.env-media-production
#   bash devops/minio/media-svcacct.sh production      # fills the two keys
#   bash devops/secrets.sh vprod dashboard-media dashboard-server/.env-media-production
#   kubectl rollout restart deployment/gbv-dashboard -n vprod
#
# ...and the same with `staging` / vstag / .env-media-staging.
#
# See documentation/secrets.md for the convention and
# planning/media-abstraction.md §4.3 / §4.7 for why these are scoped.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPE: THIS FILE HOLDS ONLY THE MEDIA KEYS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# dashboard-server's other configuration comes from the shared `gbv-bot-envs`
# secret (fed by replybot/.env-*) via envFrom, and its non-secret media settings
# -- MEDIA_BUCKET, MEDIA_PREFIX, MEDIA_PUBLIC_BASE, S3_ENDPOINT, S3_REGION,
# STORAGE_BACKEND -- are plain values in devops/values/<env>.yaml. Only the two
# credentials below are secret, and they get their own secret
# (`dashboard-media`) rather than joining gbv-bot-envs so that rotating them
# does not mean re-applying every bot credential in the platform.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A REAL VALUE HERE. The keys are left empty on purpose: they are
# written by devops/minio/media-svcacct.sh, so the credential in the cluster and
# the policy in devops/minio-media-policy.json cannot drift apart.

# The `media` bucket service account: Get/Put/Delete on media/* ONLY.
#
# This is NOT the root `minio-auth` credential, and it CANNOT reach the exports
# bucket, which holds respondent data. If you find yourself pasting root
# credentials here to "just make it work", the correct move is to re-run
# media-svcacct.sh -- a root credential in the dashboard puts every export one
# dashboard bug away from exposure.
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
